WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.140
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to be speaking
with Andrew Koppelman, who is a law professor

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.690
at Northwestern University and also author
of the book Out Today Burning Down the House

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.510
How Libertarian Philosophy Was Corrupted by
Delusion and Greed.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.110
It's not just the delusion.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.220
It's also the greed, which I think is important.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.220
Really great having you on.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.220
I appreciate.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.220
Speaker 4: That.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.220
It's a coalition.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.220
The coalition is very happy to be here.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.220
Speaker 1: So, okay.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:29.500
I mean, I think maybe the way to start talking
about this is I as a progressive on the left,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:38.130
I air on the side of small l libertarianism
when given the choice between authoritarianism

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:43.100
and libertarianism, unless there's a really
good, compelling reason to get a government

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.700
or some authority involved, I say stay out
of it.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.010
And of course, there are so many exceptions
to this that's like my general idea.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:57.281
But when we talk about libertarian philosophy
in the way you talk about in the book, you're

00:00:57.281 --> 00:01:03.370
not really talking about what I'm talking
about, which is a small l libertarian leaning.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:04.370
Is that right?

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.270
Speaker 4: Yeah, I think that's right.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:14.650
I think that the common ground between us
is the idea that the point of government,

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.750
what we should be aiming at in government
is giving everybody the means to decide for

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.620
themselves how they're going to live their
lives.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:27.630
And now that is a very new idea in human history.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:33.600
For most of human history, the point of the
state was the greater glory of King Sargon

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.430
or the triumph of the two religion, or the
triumph of the master race or, you know, something

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.390
other than the flourishing of the individual.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:48.270
So really the argument between you and me
and the Libertarians is an argument about

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:49.460
strategy.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:55.400
How does one go about building a world in
which people are free to live as they like?

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:00.830
And what's distinctive about the Libertarians
is a very strong view that the way to assure

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:04.970
that is to cripple the state, to limit the
power of the state.

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:09.711
If only the state were radically limited,
only protected our persons and property, and

00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:12.160
did nothing else, we would be freer.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.620
Speaker 1: When you talk about the delusion
part, it would be great to hear a little bit

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:18.790
about what you mean.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:24.470
You know, I've experienced conversations with
libertarians that devolve into, you know,

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:30.190
strange conversations about everybody makes
their own road and puts tolls on it.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:35.140
Or I'm sure you've heard in popular media
the way that these conversations often go.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.310
What is the delusion part that you're talking
about?

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:44.380
Speaker 4: Well, the delusion is the idea
that in order to live the lives that we want,

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:50.650
that we can do without the kind of big, powerful
state that we have had, certainly in the United

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.360
States in the last 100 years.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:02.440
Because there are quite a lot of things that
keep us from living our lives the way we want,

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:08.220
which do not consist of either the government
or other people encroaching on our property.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:15.349
I mean, one good example is COVID or COVID
didn't violate anybody's rights.

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:19.520
But the reason why we were able to beat it
back is because government raised an awful

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.270
lot of money with taxation.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.300
Libertarians hate taxation.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.520
Big government gave a lot of money to private
investors who would not have invested the

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.430
money themselves in developing a vaccine.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:37.739
And it was a partnership between government
and the private sector that made it possible

00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:42.470
for us to go back to something approaching
normal life.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:47.360
Without that, I think that we would still
be hunkered down in our houses, fearful of

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.360
death every time we are in a room with a group
of strangers.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.310
Speaker 1: Yeah, that that's really interesting.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:57.600
You know, oftentimes the word coercive comes
up in conversations with with libertarians

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.209
and, you know, whether it's applied to taxes
or business regulations or the IRS, at the

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:08.160
end of the day, if you just refuse to pay
your taxes, eventually someone arrests you

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.700
and you could potentially, you know, this
concept of coercion.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.689
It seems to me that if you step back a little
bit from politics and just think about anthropology

00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:24.050
and so many other fields of study, there's
this idea that once you get to a group larger

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:27.979
than 150 often is the number that's mentioned
150 humans.

00:04:27.979 --> 00:04:32.969
You sort of need to have some decisions made
from the top in order to be able to grow as

00:04:32.969 --> 00:04:34.900
a society beyond that.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:40.009
And so even if there is a little bit of coercion,
don't we need a little coercion in order to

00:04:40.009 --> 00:04:42.639
be able to to live as a modern society?

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:47.210
Speaker 4: Well, I think that it's a mistake
to think of it in terms of size.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:53.499
The question is not a little coercion or a
lot of coercion, but a right amount of coercion.

00:04:53.499 --> 00:04:59.539
So one example that I think that libertarian,
you know, libertarian philosophers who are

00:04:59.539 --> 00:05:00.539
who.

00:05:00.539 --> 00:05:02.280
Why engage with primarily in the book?

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.639
Because libertarianism is a political philosophy,
I think.

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:10.330
Well, if there is a minimal thing for the
state to do, it's to keep people from harming

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:11.539
other people.

00:05:11.539 --> 00:05:17.539
Well, one way in which we can harm other people
is by pollution.

00:05:17.539 --> 00:05:23.389
You and I can agree to have me make a product
that you buy, and it makes me better off and

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:24.389
it makes you better off.

00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:26.090
And that's the great thing about markets.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:30.690
But look, if in the course of manufacturing,
the thing that you want to buy, I poison all

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.949
the children in a three mile radius of my
factory then.

00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:36.180
Now I'm not adding value to the world.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:40.110
Now you and I are conspiring to hurt a bunch
of innocent people.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.180
Well, so how do you fix that?

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.949
You're not going to fix it with private lawsuits
because it's hard for each of the victims

00:05:45.949 --> 00:05:47.619
to prove that they've been harmed.

00:05:47.619 --> 00:05:52.869
The only way to do it is with regulation that
limits what you can put in the air.

00:05:52.869 --> 00:05:57.639
And if you want to have that kind of regulation,
you need a government with a huge staff of

00:05:57.639 --> 00:06:02.930
scientists to try to figure out which chemicals
are harmful and which aren't.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:05.740
You cannot fix this problem without big government.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:11.580
Speaker 1: You talk in the book, as you mention,
about figures like Robert Nozick.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.900
You talk about Hayek.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:19.719
We hear a lot about Ayn Rand and the Koch
brothers and all of these different players

00:06:19.719 --> 00:06:28.379
that are often loosely described as as libertarians
of some sort of flavor of modern libertarianism

00:06:28.379 --> 00:06:33.669
has been influenced the most by who of these
characters or others that you engage with

00:06:33.669 --> 00:06:34.669
in the book?

00:06:34.669 --> 00:06:41.969
Speaker 4: Well, the common sense libertarianism
that an awful lot of people hold, the sort

00:06:41.969 --> 00:06:47.319
of the common sense minimal state idea really
owes the most to a murray Rothbard.

00:06:47.319 --> 00:06:51.039
Who is the most important political philosopher
you never heard of.

00:06:51.039 --> 00:06:55.159
He's the one who shaped the early Libertarian
Party.

00:06:55.159 --> 00:07:00.349
He is the one who persuaded Charles Koch to
start funding libertarian causes.

00:07:00.349 --> 00:07:04.869
He is the one who met young Robert Moses when
he was a graduate student and got him interested

00:07:04.869 --> 00:07:06.129
in this stuff.

00:07:06.129 --> 00:07:14.020
He also mentor Randy Barnett, who was the
mastermind of the legal challenge to Obamacare.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.990
And so I think that he's the most important
of the lot.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:24.029
And I talk about all of these people in the
book, which basically describes the way in

00:07:24.029 --> 00:07:28.379
which libertarian thought has evolved over
time.

00:07:28.379 --> 00:07:31.099
And he is fundamentally an anarchist.

00:07:31.099 --> 00:07:40.409
But I know one way of taking his line of thought
is to that which I guess people who are not

00:07:40.409 --> 00:07:46.500
willing to go all the way to anarchism have
gone is to say, Well, whatever else we do,

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:51.499
let's minimize the power of the state and
the authority of the state.

00:07:51.499 --> 00:07:57.749
And while that's still not the official line
of the Republican Party has enormous influence

00:07:57.749 --> 00:07:58.749
there.

00:07:58.749 --> 00:08:04.330
Trump didn't talk in broadly libertarian terms,
but you look at what actually got passed when

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:09.800
the Republicans had both houses and the presidency,
they did a big tax cut for the rich.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:14.770
They narrowly took health care away from 20
million people, and they gutted administrative

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:20.529
agencies like the EPA, which I guess goes
to the thing I said at the beginning about

00:08:20.529 --> 00:08:22.969
a coalition.

00:08:22.969 --> 00:08:25.309
Libertarianism draws two different kinds of
people.

00:08:25.309 --> 00:08:32.560
One is philosophers like Nosek, who have a
story to tell about what unjust state is.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.360
And the other part of the coalition is business
people who'd like to be able to hurt people

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:38.860
without being bothered by the police.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:43.201
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, but I'm curious in
terms of the modern Republican Party, because

00:08:43.201 --> 00:08:47.290
you're sort of mentioning a little bit about
the rhetoric versus the things that were passed

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:52.700
under Trump in those those couple of years
in the modern Republican Party, kind of in

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.300
the MAGA direction that that that it has gone.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.560
It seems nationalism is a big thing.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:07.220
It seems as though authoritarianism, when
it is convenient to you, it has become an

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.690
increasingly big feature.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:15.750
Is there any real traditional libertarianism
that is still kind of left or prominent in

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.180
the modern Republican Party?

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:24.520
Speaker 4: Well, the libertarianism that remains
prominent is a desire to constrain the state.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:32.520
The formula of the Republican Party for a
long time has been to talk populist while

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:36.670
doing big favors for rich supporters.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:43.060
And so you see that in what continues to be
the target of the Republicans, the modern

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.130
administrative state.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:50.570
This administrative state is really inconvenient
for business.

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:56.060
So you tell people you tell ordinary working
people that, you know, I'm going to protect

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:59.780
you from these arrogant government bureaucrats
who don't respect you.

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:06.630
And then you limit the capacity of the bureaucrats
to deliver clean water and safe workplaces

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:08.210
and clean air.

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:14.130
And so the consequence is that then you tell
your ordinary working person, look, I saved

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:15.130
you from the bureaucrats.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:19.680
And as a consequence, that ordinary working
person does not live long enough to meet his

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:20.680
grandchildren.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:21.680
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:27.640
I mean, I guess the desire to limit the power
of the state seems to be only in ways that

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:31.340
those on what's perceived to be the left want
to have the state involved.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:32.340
Right.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:37.140
Because in many other ways, in terms of clamping
down on media outlets that you disagree with

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:41.840
or when it comes to a lot of these social
issues, abortion and gay marriage and the

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:44.540
right to fire someone for their perceived
or sexual orientation.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:48.890
In those cases, they seem okay actually using
the power of the state.

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:55.080
Speaker 4: Well, and what we saw under the
first Trump administration, we'll see if there's

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.220
a second Trump administration.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:02.370
But they've already learned that they could
get away with scooping up peaceful protesters

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:05.800
and shoving them into vans and taking through
an undisclosed location.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:08.750
That doesn't seem especially libertarian.

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:16.770
On the other hand, as I said, the libertarianism
today is a coalition between business people

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:22.420
who want to make a lot of money and these
principled libertarians.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:27.720
And it is the business people who I think
are the prevalent part of the coalition.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:31.630
And I should say I'm not casting any aspersions
on businesspeople.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:37.140
The ordinary business person in a well-regulated
state can feel pretty confident that if he's

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:42.900
making money, he's doing good for the world
because somebody is buying his product.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:47.630
And if he were injuring innocent third parties,
somebody from the government would come and

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:49.730
tell him that and tell him to stop.

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:54.700
And the fact that nobody tells him to stop
means that he can feel like he's done a good

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:56.800
thing in the world with his business.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:59.560
You will not be able to be confident of that
under Trump.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:02.340
Trump wants you to make money whether you
hurt people or not.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:06.670
Speaker 1: Last thing I wanted to touch on
during the pandemic, there were a lot of,

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:15.890
again, self-described libertarians who were
saying the vaccines, that's a problem, masks

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:17.090
a problem.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.520
What's happening in school is a problem, a
problem.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:23.650
What businesses are having to do for this
initial period of a few months that quite

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:25.210
frankly, ended pretty quickly.

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:28.090
All of these things are a problem.

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:34.550
What would a sort of more sensible but still
libertarian approach be to those issues that

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:35.730
we saw during the pandemic?

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:39.070
Speaker 4: Well, it's pretty simple.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:44.040
The the liberal response, which I think is
the right one, is if government is proposing

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:47.650
to restrict somebody's liberty, it's got to
have a pretty good reason.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:52.780
Sometimes the reasons are no good, and sometimes
the story about arrogant government bureaucrats

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:53.960
is correct.

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:58.560
The Centers for Disease Control came up with
a test that didn't work and wouldn't let anybody

00:12:58.560 --> 00:12:59.580
else develop one.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:00.740
It was a scandal.

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:06.940
But on the other hand, yeah, a lot of these
restrictions, certainly the massive promotion

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:12.640
of vaccines with tax money and the requirement
that health care workers get vaccinated.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.620
Yeah, it promotes people's liberty.

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:16.620
It keeps them alive.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:17.620
In fact.

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:23.070
Speaker 1: We have been speaking about some
of the things in Andrew Koppelman, new book

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:29.820
out today, Burning Down the House How Libertarian
Philosophy Was Corrupted by Delusion and Greed.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:33.520
We're linking to the book and I really appreciate
your time today.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:34.730
Here it is.

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:35.730
There it is.

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:36.730
A beautiful.

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:37.730
Speaker 4: Podcast.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:38.730
Speaker 1: Thanks, Andrew.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:39.730
Speaker 4: Thank you.

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:39.733
This was great.

